This file describes how to install tfem on Linux. This a generic file.
See INSTALL.fedora, INSTALL.macos for more specific info on certain systems.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------

It is assumed you are using a Linux (or Unix) distribution where the
"usual suspects" have already been installed:

    * bash
    * basic Unix utilities (sed, echo, sort, ...)
    * git
    * make
    * ctags (Universal Ctags form https://ctags.io)
    * fig2dev
    * ghostscript
    * a pdf viewer
    * ... ?


System requirements
===================

1) A fortran compiler supporting the latest standards. Recent versions of
   the following compilers (*1) are known to work:

  * Intel Fortran (*2)
  * Gfortran
  * NAG Fortran

Other compilers might work as well, but you will be on your own.

2) An optimized BLAS/LAPACK library, such as MKL (*3) or OpenBLAS (*4).

3) A complete LaTeX installation to build the userguide.

4) (Option) An external mesh generator. For example gmsh.

5) (Option) An external visualization program. For example paraview.

NOTES:
 *1 A new tfem revision is only tested against the most recent released
    versions of the three mentioned compilers.
 *2 Only the new ifx Intel Compiler is supported. Do not use ifort anymore.
 *3 See file README.MKL for more instructions on using MKL.
 *4 Use the system provided version of OpenBLAS, where possible. See file
    README.OpenBLAS for instructions on compiling the library yourself.


Installing TFEM from a fresh checkout tree
==========================================

(See Userguide how to check out a fresh TFEM tree using git).

1) Create a file Mdefs.mk in the root of the tree to define the Makefile macros.

Copy the file Mdefs.mk from Mdefs_distr.mk:

  cp Mdefs_distr.mk Mdefs.mk

and edit Mdefs.mk to represent your system (libraries/compiler(s)/options).

NOTE: If you do not have or have not compiled the HSL libary (see below),
you need to add the -DNO_LIBHSL flag to the FPPFLAGS macro in the Mdefs.mk file.

2) You need a linear equation solver. The default solver is MA41 from the
HSL2002 library, however you need a license for that. The department of
Mechanical Engineering of the TU/e has an academic licence and the source
files can be obtained from Martien Hulsen (m.a.hulsen@tue.nl). Unpack the
hsl2002.tar.gz file into the tfem root directory by using the commands:

  cd <root-of-tfem>
  tar zxf <path_of_hsl2002.tar.gz>/hsl2002.tar.gz

and type

  make libhsl
  make libhsldirclean

The documentation is in the directory doc and can be viewed with the tfdoc
command.

NOTE: If you do not have a license for HSL, you can use other solvers. See the
userguide.

3) Compile/build TFEM. Type:

  make all

The target 'all' will build the core, some common addons, basic physics add-ons
and the userguide. Note: the target 'all' is the default target, so typing
just 'make' is also sufficient is this case.

Some computational nodes/machines do not provide LaTeX and

  make allc

will build everything 'all' does, except for the userguide.

NOTE: If you do not have a license for HSL or you will use another linear solver
and did not compile libhsl, type

  make all libhsldummy

The interface to HSL (the add-on hsl) is still compiled, but a dummy hsl
library is created to resolve the missing symbols.


Installing additional add-ons
==============================

Although the name suggest otherwise: not all add-ons are installed for the
target 'all'. To install additional add-ons type

  make all <add-on-name(s)>

If the tfem tree has already been build using `make all', the target 'all' can
be omitted. It is however recommended to always include the 'all' target to be
sure it is up to date.

An alternative for adding add-ons to the arguments of make is setting an
environment variable TFEMADDONS. Examples:

  csh:

    setenv TFEMADDONS "viscoelastic inertia"

  bash:

    export TFEMADDONS="viscoelastic bcf diffuse_interface elastic \
    viscoelastic_integration inertia generalized_stokes"

The add-ons specified in TFEMADDONS will be added to "all". So typing just
"make" will include compilation of the additional add-ons.

If you want to include all "physics" related add-ons in one go, type:

  make all physics

You can install other add-ons (like solvers etc.) by adding them to the
command line or including these in the environment variable TFEMADDONS.
Similarly, you can use

  make allc physics

to exclude the LaTeX documentation.


Updating TFEM to a new version
==============================

Use git to update your tree (see userguide). It is advised to always
rebuild the whole tree to get rid of possible incompatibilities between new
and previously compiled routines. Therefore type in the root of the tfem tree:

  make clean
  make all <add-on-name(s)>

Rebuild your own code also after the update.


Rebuilding the HSL solver library
=================================

The HSL solver library is usually not updated and recompiled. However, it is
a good idea to rebuild the library if your compiler has been updated to a newer
version. For the HSL library this amounts to

make libhslclean
make libhsl


Dummy libraries
===============

If you do not have external libraries installed (for example HSL or Lapack), you
will get unresolved symbols while building an executable. You can build dummy
symbols by typing

  make libhsldummy

for HSL and

  make liblapackdummy

for Lapack.


Developers
==========

If you are developer of sources in the tfem tree run from the root of the
tree:

  make developer

